## The Kitchen ((+ The Kitchen))
### “The Banquet That Never Ends”
)

/

![kitchen_scene](https://github.com/gsimon2/nightmares-of-newborn-maner/blob/main/images/scene_art/kitchen.png?raw=true)

/

Unlike the rest of the manor, the Kitchen is almost unsettlingly clean.  
The air is warm and thick with the comforting scents of roasted meats, baked bread, and sweet pastries.  
At the center of the island counter, a perfectly roasted pig rests on a silver platter, surrounded by an absurd variety of dishes — dozens of meals, cuisines, and flavors, all carefully prepared, all untouched.  
But beneath it all lies the faint tang of exhaustion.

The space hums with quiet magic.  
Copper pots gleam on their hooks.  
The counters are spotless, yet crowded with dish after dish — stews, roasts, puddings, and purées — all steaming and fresh as though they were finished moments ago.  
Every utensil moves of its own accord: knives slice with mechanical rhythm, spoons stir endlessly, and oven doors swing open and shut without a hand to guide them.  
Despite the constant flurry of motion, not a single crumb touches the floor.

But the room feels… *tired.*  
The magic that animates it thrums weakly, like a heartbeat that’s been racing too long.  
There’s a sense of obligation in the way the pots bubble and the knives chop — a relentless routine born of desperation.  
You can feel that this kitchen has been trying, endlessly, to satisfy an unseen infant’s hunger — a baby who never seems full, never knows what it wants, and never stops crying for more.  
So the kitchen keeps cooking.  
Everything.  
Always.

|

rules (
## Searching the Banquet
